\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A quarter-turn of based edge fields}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take complex scalar coefficients and trivial links on a grid of
periods at least three. A [based one-cochain](fdip-00SH) assigns
#{c_j(y)} to the oriented edge from #{y} to #{y+e_j}. The reverse
edge has the negative value. In a translation block write
#{c_j(y)=\alpha_r(y)v_j}. For a
[period-preserving signed permutation](fdip-0164) #{b}, suppose
#{b e_j=s e_l}, where #{s=\pm1}. Let #{b} act on cochains by
evaluation on the inverse image of the oriented edge. For #{s=1}
the inverse image starts at #{b^{-1}y} in direction #{j}.
For #{s=-1} it is the reverse of the forward edge based at
#{b^{-1}y-e_j}, as in \ref{fdip-00PY}. Thus the component at
#{y} in direction #{l} is
##{(b c)_l(y)=\alpha_{br}(y)
\begin{cases}
v_j,&s=1,\\
-\alpha_r(-e_j)v_j,&s=-1.
\end{cases}}
At a general label the reversed component carries the character
of a shifted base point.}

\p{Now take distinct axes #{a,b,c}, with equal periods in the
first two, and a label supported on axis #{c}. Use the rotation
sending #{e_a} to #{-e_b} and #{e_b} to #{e_a}, fixing the other
axes. It fixes the label and its two transverse character values
are one. The transverse coefficients transform as
#{(v_a,v_b)\mapsto(v_b,-v_a)}. The vectors with coefficient
pairs #{(1,\ui)} and #{(1,-\ui)} have multipliers #{\ui}
and #{-\ui}. Every other coordinate has multiplier one.
For the [cyclic grading](fdip-016I) with #{\ell=4} and
#{\zeta=\ui}, the transverse degrees are #{1} and #{-1},
and the axial component has degree zero.}
